WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Film Courage: What are stakes?

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.580
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher and&nbsp;
Consultant: Something that confuses a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.240
writers, stakes are very generally&nbsp;
like whatever hangs in the balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:20.880
with regard to what your character is trying to&nbsp;
achieve. A lot of movies are very clear external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:28.380
life and death stakes. If your character doesn't&nbsp;
escape the serial killer then they will die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:36.300
your protagonist will die, so that is life and&nbsp;
death stakes. It's whatever consequence would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.040
come to bear if your protagonist fails to achieve&nbsp;
their story goal. That's how I would define it.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.680
Film Courage: Why are stakes important?

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.080
Naomi: Well I think stakes&nbsp;
are important because that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:53.760
if there's nothing at stake then the story doesn't&nbsp;
matter. If your protagonist is trying to achieve a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:01.860
thing but nothing there won't be any consequence&nbsp;
to it then I don't know if your audience will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:10.260
remain engaged in the story right because if&nbsp;
we understand that a series of events doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.120
matter it doesn't have any consequence to it it's&nbsp;
inconsequential I think most of us would check out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.220
could be entertaining for a while but eventually&nbsp;
we'll be like okay I I don't really care about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:26.460
ending of it so whatever's at stake in the story&nbsp;
is really what gets us to care about the outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.700
yeah and I know again we use hustle as an example&nbsp;
and I think that's what got the film for me that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.040
why I liked it I'm not really into sports but&nbsp;
the the stakes that were involved and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.840
numerous times yeah that was what was so great is&nbsp;
you thought you got through one hurdle and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.540
there was another one presented yeah and I think&nbsp;
all of these elements go hand in hand right they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.040
all depend on each other to create the effect&nbsp;
that you want them to create on the audience so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.760
what's great about Hustle is like it's not life or&nbsp;
death stakes it's not that high stakes of a of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.100
movie but you feel so much for that character&nbsp;
that what's important to him is important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:11.820
you so that what this career means to him it's the&nbsp;
only thing he's ever done it's what he loves it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:17.520
what he's dedicated his whole life to there's the&nbsp;
feeling that if he washes out now his whole life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.400
will be meaningless or he'll have invested all&nbsp;
this time and something that ultimately didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.760
matter had no out no consequence so all all of&nbsp;
that is wrapped up in what's at stake for him so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.140
what I was describing earlier the external&nbsp;
stakes of like what's the consequence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:39.480
will happen if your character fails right that's&nbsp;
usually in a movie there's something external at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:46.920
stake so life and death stakes or like somebody's&nbsp;
safety or losing the house or what have you but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.900
then there's also the internal stakes of it the&nbsp;
emotional stakes of it which is really like what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:59.940
it means to your character so it's important for&nbsp;
the Adam Sandler character to achieve that goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:05.340
because of all that stuff that it represents to&nbsp;
him and that's that I think is the more emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.820
side of things and that's what really gets us&nbsp;
like emotionally invested in a story right I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.020
think there's like one scene in the beginning&nbsp;
or close to it where Adam Sandler's on the phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.760
with someone he's like 50 year old guys don't have&nbsp;
dreams we have nightmares and panic attacks yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.640
yeah they do such a good job of like layering&nbsp;
that stuff in right of of just exactly what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.280
it mean if he fails to get this last career&nbsp;
thing because it would be very easy for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:38.940
movie to hit all of the the plot beats that&nbsp;
we expect but to feel empty right like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.220
would have been the bad version of that movie of&nbsp;
like a near retirement guy has one last chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.500
to get a player signed and you could see that&nbsp;
playing out in a way that the plot would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:56.400
sense but because they went the extra the extra&nbsp;
mile in terms of like developing that character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.900
and what it all meant to him that made it I&nbsp;
loved that movie it made it very emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.780
for me instead of just being like oh this is a&nbsp;
fun movie about guys playing basketball it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:13.320
much more about the Adam Sandler character and&nbsp;
that was like a redemption story for him it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.540
like my life hasn't been a waste I'm not too old&nbsp;
to accomplish things all of those things I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.160
were layered into that movie and that's what made&nbsp;
it that's what actually I made it a movie that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:29.100
would recommend to people versus one that I watch&nbsp;
and forget about and and then without giving away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:35.640
too much the basketball player has his own stakes&nbsp;
and then you also see that part of the stakes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:41.280
Adam Sandler is proving to the basketball player&nbsp;
that I'm actually not going to let you down yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.680
that's what was really great about that movie too&nbsp;
is that relationship between those two guys so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:53.880
it's very much an underdog sports movie and the&nbsp;
two of them are the only the only two the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.880
person that each has in their life that really&nbsp;
believes in them in a way that that matters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:05.520
they can help each other and so there are two&nbsp;
underdogs working together to achieve this big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.860
thing that nobody thinks they can achieve so yes&nbsp;
that that made me root for them what I love that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.480
two underdogs working together that's right yeah&nbsp;
yeah that's what works about it how do we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:20.820
the stakes are high enough for the audience to&nbsp;
be emotionally invested that's a good it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.560
good question and I think that's one of those&nbsp;
things that's a little bit trial and error in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:33.660
your in your writing you do what you can to create&nbsp;
the stakes in your story external and internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.720
try to plan them out in a way that that you feel&nbsp;
like will be enough to get the audience invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:45.900
but that is definitely one of those things that&nbsp;
in completing the first draft sometimes you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.620
somebody read it they're like I don't really&nbsp;
understand why this matters or I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:54.120
know why I should care about this story and&nbsp;
then that the stakes aren't coming through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:59.520
right they may be high enough but they may&nbsp;
just not be conveyed on the page in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:07.080
that the whoever read it can understand why they&nbsp;
should care so there's both the aspect of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:14.940
planning as you're developing your story thinking&nbsp;
through what's at stake and why this is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:19.380
matter and what it means to the character and&nbsp;
so that's why the audience will care about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.300
thinking through all of those questions and and&nbsp;
making sure that's in your story that's half the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.500
battle and then the other half is actually&nbsp;
figuring out how to put it on the page in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:33.900
way that your reader can pick it up right and&nbsp;
can understand and can feel what's at stake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.640
yeah I think that was what was my problem with&nbsp;
Bridesmaids when I heard about it and it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

